<x-layouts.main title="About" description="Theo Marsh — design engineer at Foldwork, writing about small tools from a flat in Bristol." current="/about">
    <x-sections.page-intro heading="About" />
    <x-sections.prose body="<p>I am Theo. I design and build developer tools, which mostly means I spend my days on the parts of software that other people have to use in order to do their real work.</p><p>I came at it sideways. I trained as a graphic designer, spent two years in an agency setting type for things I did not believe in, and learned to program because a print job needed four hundred variations and I was not going to make them by hand. That is still roughly how I decide what to build: something is tedious, the tedium has a shape, and the shape can be described to a computer.</p><h2>What I care about</h2><p>Tools that are small enough to understand completely. Interfaces that tell you the truth about what they are doing. Software you can still open in ten years because it did not need a server. None of that is a philosophy — it is just what I have found to be true after shipping enough things that were none of those and watching them rot.</p><h2>This site</h2><p>Everything here is written by hand and generated by <a href='/projects/halfmoon'>Halfmoon</a>, which is six hundred lines of Go I can read in an afternoon. There is no analytics, no newsletter, and no comment section. If something here is wrong or useful, tell me — the address is on the homepage.</p>" />
    <x-sections.background heading="The short version" />
    <x-sections.link-group :links="$site->elsewhere_links" heading="Elsewhere" note="Email is the one I actually read." />
</x-layouts.main>
